# ObservableToTaskBridgeSites.allow — the SEEDED inventory of `.ToTask(` call sites, for
# ObservableToTaskBridgeGuard. One `relative/path.cs<TAB>count` per line; `#` starts a comment.
#
# 🚨 THIS LIST MAY ONLY SHRINK. Adding a line is not a fix — the guard fails on a new file, a
# raised count, or a raised TOTAL, and TotalBudget in the guard must be lowered by the same
# amount whenever an entry is deleted or lowered.
#
# It covers ONLY the trees whose sweep is still in flight: `test/` and `memex/`.
# `src/`, `tools/`, `samples/` and `clients/` are held at ZERO with NO allow file at all
# (maintainer, 2026-08-30: "no ToTask ever"; "in src especially we should have zero"), so there
# is deliberately no line here you could add for them. When a wave empties one of the two roots
# above, move it into the guard's ProductionRoots and delete its lines here.
#
# Counts are produced by the guard's own scanner, with comments and string literals masked — so a
# doc comment quoting the banned shape (this repo does that at length, on purpose, to explain WHY
# it is banned) is not a site and must never be edited to satisfy a textual scan.
#
# 🚨 SEED AGAINST main's TIP, NOT YOUR BRANCH POINT. CI checks out the MERGE REF (main merged
# into the PR head), so a file main gained after you branched is present when the guard runs but
# absent from a seed taken locally — and the guard correctly reports it as a NEW SITE, reddening
# a PR whose own diff never touched it. That is not a false positive; it is the seed being stale.
# This file was re-seeded for exactly that reason after main gained
# PodHubNotHereRefusalTest.cs and RecycledHubRefusalTest.cs mid-review. Merge main, re-run the
# seed, and lower TotalBudget to match.
#
# The sweep PRs that only REMOVE sites deliberately do NOT touch this file: the guard reports a
# shrunken count as `STALE (please tidy)` rather than failing, so concurrent batches never
# collide here. One tidy PR reconciles the inventory at the end.

# The waves have landed. `memex/` reached ZERO and has moved into the guard's ProductionRoots,
# so it can no longer appear here at all. What remains in `test/` is TWO deliberate negative
# controls in one file: DisposalWaitBridgeTest measures what the OLD bridge does (resumes its
# caller inline on the signalling thread) — it must keep using `.ToTask()` or it measures
# nothing. The mechanism twin, InlineResumptionMechanismTest, is the guard's one hard-coded
# exemption and needs no line here.
test/MeshWeaver.Messaging.Hub.Test/DisposalWaitBridgeTest.cs	2
